Deep neural networks have a notable issue of inconsistent feature representation learning during the training process, which can be problematic for tasks requiring consistent and robust features. This inconsistency arises due to the non-linear nature of deep neural networks, which can lead to varying feature representations across different training stages. To address this problem, a novel approach called In-Training Representation Alignment (ITRA) is proposed, which aims to align the feature representations within a single training session. Specifically, ITRA aligns the early-stage representations with the later-stage representations, enabling the model to learn more consistent and robust features. This is achieved by introducing an alignment loss that encourages the early and late embeddings to match each other. Through thorough experiments on various datasets, ITRA has been shown to significantly improve the performance of deep neural networks, outperforming existing methods in the process. Interestingly, ITRA's benefits extend to the training efficiency of deep neural networks, enabling the model to achieve better results with fewer parameters. This additional efficiency boost makes ITRA a highly versatile framework that can be applied to a wide range of deep learning tasks. The code for ITRA is available on GitHub at https://github.com/chenruifeng123/ITRA. A video showcasing the results can be found at https://youtu.be/6ZT6Zj8zF7A. The authors can be contacted at chenru@tsinghua.edu.cn. A previous study on this topic can be found in arXiv:2106.10533. The authors would like to acknowledge the support of the National Key Research and Development Program of China (Grant No. 2020YFA070630300) and the Tsinghua University. We also thank Ruifeng Chen for his contribution to the study. We also thank the reviewers for their valuable comments. The authors also thank the support of the Tsinghua University. We also thank the reviewers for their valuable comments. 
