A significant challenge in AI research involves developing machines that can effectively identify and counter disinformation. To address this issue, we introduce PropaNews - a comprehensive dataset comprising 3.1 million labeled news articles, annotated with various types of disinformation. Our dataset outperforms existing resources by a significant margin, with an 8-fold increase in size and a 50% boost in the number of unique disinformation instances. We utilize PropaNews to train advanced detectors and generative models, and surprisingly, we find that the latter perform better despite being trained on unmarked data. Furthermore, our results indicate that the models trained on PropaNews deliver significantly improved disinformation detection and mitigation capabilities, with improved F1 scores of 5.1% and 7.4% respectively, when compared to state-of-the-art models. Additionally, we find that the models trained on PropaNews exhibit improved generalizability and robustness compared to models trained on synthetic data. Our PropaNews dataset is freely accessible for research purposes. 
