This study delves into the connection between control theory and geometric properties of convex sets, focusing on the support function and its applications. We begin by exploring how the support function varies under controlled system transformations, then apply this understanding to examine invariance of convex sets under control systems and bound constraints. Furthermore, we utilize the support function to derive tight limits on the support function of invariant convex sets, ultimately leading to a discussion of the relationship between support functions and control Lyapunov functions in controlled systems. We also investigate the support function's role in stabilizing convex domains and discuss its implications for control Lyapunov functions. In addition, we provide a detailed example illustrating a stabilization function that does not conform to the conventional Lyapunov function criteria, while also touching on the support function's connection to optimal control and dynamic programming. We conclude the study by highlighting the support function's utility in solving the minimum energy problem for convex domains. In the appendices, we provide a detailed derivation of the support function's properties and provide additional insights into the relationship between stabilization and convex domains. We also discuss the support function's applications in solving the minimum energy problem for convex domains. The results presented in this study are intended to be a stepping stone for further research into the field of geometric control theory. We believe that this study will provide a valuable resource for researchers interested in exploring the intersection of control theory and geometry. The accompanying code, available at \url{https://github.com/haykelb/convexdomain}, allows users to experiment with the support function and invariant convex sets, while providing a starting point for further investigation into the study of controlled convex domains. The code, written